  "content": {
    "id": "p23-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "The maximum number of daylight hours a particular town receives is 14.5 hours. This occurs on a day known as the summer solstice. The minimum number of daylight hours the town receives is 9.25, and this occurs six months later. The number of daylight hours in the town $t$ months after the summer solstice can be modelled by the function:\n$$D(t)=a\\cos(nt)+b.$$\nFind the values of $a$, $b$ and $n$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=2.625,\\quad b=11.875,\\quad n=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$.",
      "worked": "The model starts at its maximum, so $a$ is positive. The period is $12$ months.\n$$\\begin{align*}\na&=\\frac{14.5-9.25}{2}\\\\\na&=2.625\\\\\nb&=\\frac{14.5+9.25}{2}\\\\\nb&=11.875\\\\\nn&=\\frac{2\\pi}{12}\\\\\nn&=\\frac{\\pi}{6}.\n\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 42
  },
